# mktodo Project Principles

## Code Quality

### Static Analysis Compliance
- All code MUST pass `go vet` without warnings
- All code MUST pass `golangci-lint` checks with no exceptions
- Linting failures block merges to main branch

### Code Reviews
- All changes require peer review before merge
- Reviewers verify adherence to these principles
- Security-sensitive changes require additional scrutiny

### Documentation
- Public functions and types MUST have godoc comments
- Complex algorithms MUST include explanatory comments
- README.md reflects current functionality and usage

### Error Handling
- Errors MUST be handled explicitly, never silently ignored
- Error messages MUST be clear and actionable for users
- Use wrapped errors to preserve context (`fmt.Errorf` with `%w`)

## Code Simplicity

### Avoid Unnecessary Complexity
- Prefer simple, straightforward solutions over clever ones
- Extract complex logic into well-named functions
- Keep functions focused on single responsibilities
- Limit cyclomatic complexity (aim for < 10 per function)

### Readable Code
- Use descriptive variable and function names
- Prefer clarity over brevity in naming
- Avoid deeply nested conditionals (max 3 levels)
- Limit function length (aim for < 50 lines)

### Dependencies
- Minimize external dependencies
- Preferred libraries only (Cobra, Viper, Bubbletea as specified)
- Justify any new dependency additions
- Regularly update dependencies for security

### Text Block Management
- Defer all text blocks to `github.com/jvzantvoort/mktodo/messages`
- Use `go:embed` construct for message templates
- Keep UI strings separated from business logic

## Testing Standards

### Test Coverage
- Aim for high test coverage (target: 80%+ overall)
- All new features MUST include tests
- All bug fixes MUST include regression tests
- Critical paths require 100% coverage

### Test Quality
- Tests MUST be clear, maintainable, and focused
- One logical assertion per test when possible
- Use table-driven tests for multiple scenarios
- Mock external dependencies appropriately

### Test Types
- Unit tests for individual functions and types
- Integration tests for component interactions
- End-to-end tests for complete workflows
- TUI components tested with Bubbletea testing utilities

### Continuous Integration
- All tests MUST pass before merge
- Tests run automatically on every commit
- GitHub workflows enforce quality gates
- Performance regression tests for critical operations

### Test Organization
- Tests live in `*_test.go` files alongside source
- Use meaningful test function names: `Test<Function>_<Scenario>`
- Share test fixtures appropriately
- Clean up test artifacts (files, state)

## User Experience Consistency

### Command Line Interface
- Follow Unix conventions and standards
- Provide consistent flag naming across commands
- Support both short (`-p`) and long (`--project`) flags
- Include helpful error messages with usage hints

### Interactive TUI
- Consistent keybindings across all views
- Visual feedback for all user actions
- Clear indication of current state and mode
- Graceful handling of terminal resize

### Error Messages
- User-facing errors MUST be clear and actionable
- Suggest fixes when possible
- Distinguish user errors from system errors
- No stack traces in normal operation

### Output Formatting
- Consistent formatting across all commands
- Support for both human-readable and machine-parseable output
- Respect terminal width and capabilities
- Use color/formatting appropriately (degrades gracefully)

### Prompts and Confirmations
- Confirm destructive operations (delete, etc.)
- Provide `--yes` or `--force` flags to skip prompts
- Clear indication of default choices
- Timeout or cancelable for all prompts

### Help and Documentation
- Every command has `--help` output
- Help text follows consistent format
- Examples included for complex operations
- Man pages or equivalent documentation

### Accessibility
- Support non-color output modes
- Keyboard navigation for all TUI features
- Screen reader friendly where applicable
- Respect terminal capabilities

## File Handling

### Git Integration
- Always operate within git repository boundaries
- Clear error when run outside git repo
- Respect `.gitignore` patterns
- Never commit sensitive data

### Markdown Preservation
- Only modify TODO sections in markdown files
- Preserve all other content exactly
- Maintain original formatting and spacing
- Handle edge cases gracefully (malformed markdown)

### Configuration
- `.mktodo.yml` is the single source of truth
- Validate configuration on load
- Provide clear errors for invalid config
- Support sensible defaults

### File Safety
- Prompt before creating new files
- Respect command-line override flags (`--create`, etc.)
- Atomic writes to prevent corruption
- Backup or version control integration

## Performance

### Responsiveness
- CLI commands respond in < 100ms for typical operations
- TUI updates feel instantaneous (< 50ms)
- Large files (> 10K lines) handled efficiently
- Progress indicators for slow operations

### Resource Usage
- Minimize memory footprint
- Clean up resources properly (close files, etc.)
- No memory leaks in long-running TUI sessions
- Efficient parsing and processing

## Backwards Compatibility

### Configuration Format
- Maintain compatibility with existing `.mktodo.yml` files
- Provide migration tools for breaking changes
- Version configuration format if needed
- Deprecation warnings before removal

### Command Interface
- Don't break existing command-line usage
- Deprecate before removing commands/flags
- Provide migration path in deprecation messages
- Semantic versioning for releases
